{#- Each highlight tab's one plain sentence and its "Exact rules" fold: one macro per `highlights.Tab.key`, called
    with `r`, the thresholds `html._rule_values` formats. Edit a list's wording here; its screen is in screens.py.
    The rules are short bullets, one theme each, jargon glossed on first use (user's choice, Nov 3, 2026). -#}
{% macro rules(summary) %}<p class="list-summary">{{ summary }}</p>
    <details class="explain rules"><summary>Exact rules</summary><div class="chart-instruction">{{ caller() }}</div></details>{% endmacro %}

{% macro cheap_and_good(r) %}{% call rules("Companies that are both cheap and good, ranked by the weaker of the two.") %}<ul>
  <li>The top 20 by the worse of each company's Value and Quality positions{{ r.among }}: cheap and good at once, not one making up for the other.</li>
  <li>Ties go to the better sum of the two positions.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro cheapest(r) %}{% call rules("The cheapest companies for what one share earns in a normal year, whatever their quality. Many are cheap for a reason: most of them fall short on the moat and management grade.") %}<ul>
  <li>The 20 highest Value scores: the normalised owner earnings one share owns (the cash it earns after everything the business needs to stay as it is), projected 3 years on its own steady trend, declines charged, over today's price.</li>
  <li>Cyclicals at mid-cycle earnings, with no trend.</li>
  <li>Profitable operating companies only: banks, insurers, holdings and REITs are in the By kind lists.</li>
  <li>Quality is not considered.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro best_businesses(r) %}{% call rules("The best businesses by returns on capital and per-share growth, whatever their price.") %}<ul>
  <li>The 20 highest Quality scores: return on capital (ROIC; return on equity for financials) at its normal level, adjusted for its trend, shrunk when unsteady and cut for heavy debt.</li>
  <li>Plus how much one share of the business grew and whether the share count shrank.</li>
  <li>Profitable operating companies only (banks, insurers, holdings and REITs are in the By kind lists). Price is not considered.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro compounders(r) %}{% call rules("Businesses with high returns that grow smoothly without issuing shares, at a price that already pays: companies to hold for twenty years. Two routes in, one list.") %}<ul>
  <li><strong>Steady route:</strong> normal return on capital (ROIC) of {{ r.operating_min_roic }}% or more and owner earnings of at least {{ r.operating_min_yield }}% of the price; over at least {{ r.min_years }} annual reports, in the company's own currency, revenue up every year (one flat year within 2% allowed), return on capital and operating margin never down more than {{ r.operating_max_dip }}% in a year and no lower than where they started; the share count never up; outside the industries left out below.</li>
  <li><strong>Fast route:</strong> revenue per share compounding at {{ r.fast_min_revenue }}% a year or more and owner earnings per share at {{ r.fast_min_growth }}% a year or more, in US dollars, both discounted for any bumps; owner earnings of at least {{ r.fast_min_yield }}% of the price; revenue and operating margin up every year, one flat year allowed; the share count stable or falling every year; no biotechnology.</li>
  <li>Both routes: owner earnings per share positive and close to their rising trend; no new shares since the last annual report; no banks, insurers or holdings.</li>
  <li>Growth that earns its keep: at least {{ r.list_min_incremental }}% earned, in US terms, on the capital added since the oldest annual report.</li>
  <li>Pricing power: the gross margin now no more than {{ r.max_margin_loss }} points below where it was in the year of the highest inflation, so the costs were passed on.</li>
  <li>Not cyclical, not at peak margins, past the AI screen. Through recessions: its dividend per share not cut by more than {{ r.recession_fall }}% in a past global recession ({{ r.recessions }}, and {{ r.downturns }}; {{ r.recession_exempt }}, whose supervisors suspended dividends). A company whose dividend record reaches no recession passes, except where a recession hits hard (the industries Boring companies names): there only an industry leader holding net cash.</li>
  <li>It keeps only the best {{ r.top }} of the combined ranking, in that order.</li>
</ul>
<details><summary>Industries left out of the steady route</summary><p>Biotech and speciality or generic drugs, airlines, carmakers, telecoms, solar and renewable utilities, oil and gas producers, steel, aluminium, copper, farm inputs, clothing, footwear, department stores, speciality retail, restaurants, paper, regional banks, and the industries most exposed to AI (software, IT services, consulting, staffing, advertising, publishing, education, business services, internet content, gaming, health information, entertainment, broadcasting).</p></details>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro cannibals(r) %}{% call rules("Companies buying back a large part of their own shares each year, paid from the cash they make.") %}<ul>
  <li>Retiring more than {{ r.cannibal_min_reduction }}% of their shares a year, actually bought back (not a share consolidation).</li>
  <li>Paid from their own money: the buybacks of the stored years within their free cash flow plus the spare cash they hold.</li>
  <li>Value &ge; {{ r.cannibal_min_value }}, Quality &ge; {{ r.cannibal_min_quality }}, positive return on capital.</li>
  <li>Not cyclical: an upswing pays for buybacks at the top.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro below_book(r) %}{% call rules("Companies better valued on their balance sheet: priced at or below what their assets are worth, or growing into it within a few years. Operating companies and holdings.") %}<ul>
  <li>Operating companies: tangible book per share (net debt counted against it) at or above the share price, book not shrinking; or at least {{ r.asset_min_nav }}% of it and growing fast enough to pass it within {{ r.asset_horizon }} years. Bounded debt, free cash flow positive in most years, Quality &ge; 35. In the best {{ r.top }} of the combined ranking.</li>
  <li>Holdings: their book per share with its goodwill, since leaving goodwill out would value the companies a holding bought at zero. Equity at least 6% of assets, Quality &ge; 50 (too few holdings for a best fifth of their own).</li>
  <li>Both: Value &ge; 50, positive returns and earnings power, no dilution.</li>
  <li>Ordered by a balance-sheet score: the discount to book (35%), net cash (25%), shares bought back (20%) and book growth (20%).</li>
  <li>Not here: banks and insurers (their own lists) and REITs (the Real estate list: a REIT's book is cost under US accounting, fair value under international rules, so book does not compare).</li>
</ul><p>Net current assets (current assets less every liability) per share are shown beside it (left blank for banks, insurers and property developers whose land sits in current assets), and marked with a <strong>net-net</strong> pill when they cover the whole share price.</p>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro banks(r) %}{% call rules("Banks and lenders judged against other banks and lenders only: return on equity, book and capital.") %}<ul>
  <li>Banks, credit card and consumer lenders, brokers, mortgage lenders and mortgage REITs. Not in the rankings: a lender's earnings rest on borrowed money, so its yield and returns do not compare with an operating company's.</li>
  <li>The best {{ r.top }} of banks and lenders by the worse of their Value and Quality positions among them, in that order.</li>
  <li>Value: net income one share owns against its price, among banks. Quality: return on equity restated to a standard 8% capital, its steadiness, return on assets, book growth per share and buybacks.</li>
  <li>Equity at least {{ r.equity }}% of assets; no dividend cut in the last twelve months. Its dividend through 2009 is shown, not tested (most lenders cut then; 2020 is not judged, as supervisors suspended bank dividends).</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro insurers(r) %}{% call rules("Insurers judged against other insurers only: return on equity, book and capital.") %}<ul>
  <li>Life, property and casualty, specialty insurers and reinsurers. Not in the rankings: an insurer invests its clients' premiums, so its yield and returns do not compare with an operating company's.</li>
  <li>The best {{ r.top }} of insurers by the worse of their Value and Quality positions among them, in that order.</li>
  <li>Value: net income one share owns against its price, at mid-cycle (investment gains are not separated), among insurers. Quality: return on equity, its steadiness, book growth per share and buybacks.</li>
  <li>Equity at least {{ r.equity }}% of assets; no dividend cut in the last twelve months. Its dividend through 2009 is shown, not tested.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro real_estate(r) %}{% call rules("REITs judged against other REITs only: the cash one share earns, returns, and debt they can carry.") %}<ul>
  <li>Equity REITs (property owners). Not in the rankings: their book is cost under US accounting and fair value under international rules, and they are financed long, so neither compares with an operating company's.</li>
  <li>The best {{ r.top }} of REITs by the worse of their Value and Quality positions among them, in that order. Value is the owner yield: cash after the upkeep of the buildings (capex up to depreciation), close to what REITs call AFFO.</li>
  <li>Owner yield positive; interest covered at least {{ r.reit_cover }}&times; and net debt at most {{ r.reit_net_debt }}&times; operating profit: a REIT far below book with its interest barely covered is usually a debt trap.</li>
  <li>The dividend not cut by more than {{ r.recession_fall }}% in a past recession ({{ r.recessions }}), nor in the last twelve months: a REIT pays out most of its income, so its dividend follows the business. A record too short to reach a recession passes.</li>
  <li>Book / price is shown, not tested.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro utilities(r) %}{% call rules("Utilities judged against other utilities only: the cash one share earns, returns, and debt they can carry.") %}<ul>
  <li>Electric, gas and water utilities, power producers and renewables. Not in the rankings: their returns are regulated or contracted and their long-lived assets are financed with debt, so neither compares with an operating company's.</li>
  <li>The best {{ r.top }} of utilities by the worse of their Value and Quality positions among them, in that order. Value is the owner yield; Quality the return on capital, per-share growth and shares shrinking, each against utilities only.</li>
  <li>Owner yield positive; interest covered at least {{ r.utility_cover }}&times; and net debt at most {{ r.utility_net_debt }}&times; operating profit.</li>
  <li>The dividend not cut by more than {{ r.recession_fall }}% in a past recession ({{ r.recessions }}), nor in the last twelve months. A record too short to reach a recession passes.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro energy(r) %}{% call rules("Energy companies with mid-cycle returns and a balance sheet that survives a price fall. They stay in the rankings; this is a clean view of them.") %}<ul>
  <li>Yahoo's Energy sector: oil and gas producers, integrated companies, pipelines, refiners, services and drilling, coal and uranium.</li>
  <li>Value &ge; {{ r.energy_value }}, Quality &ge; {{ r.energy_quality }}, normal return on capital (ROIC) &ge; {{ r.energy_roic }}%.</li>
  <li>Net cash, or net debt at most {{ r.net_debt }}&times; operating profit or interest covered {{ r.cover }}&times;, and at most {{ r.net_debt_equity }}% of equity.</li>
  <li>Not at peak margins (most of energy is cyclical and valued at mid-cycle earnings). Yahoo keeps only about four years of reports, which include the 2022 price spike, so a mid-cycle figure may still read high: the debt test is the guard.</li>
  <li>The dividend not cut by more than {{ r.recession_fall }}% through a downturn ({{ r.recessions }}, and the oil price collapse of {{ r.downturns }}), nor in the last twelve months. A company that pays no dividend has no record and passes.</li>
  <li>In combined rank order, not cut to the best {{ r.top }} (too few would pass).</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro net_cash(r) %}{% call rules("Companies whose spare cash is a large part of their market value: a buyer gets the cash with the business.") %}<ul>
  <li>Net cash (cash less all debt, on the shareholders' share) of {{ r.net_cash_min }}% or more of the market value.</li>
  <li>Shares not issued and free cash flow positive in at least {{ r.net_cash_min_fcf }}% of the years, so the pile is neither diluted nor burned.</li>
  <li>Banks, insurers and holdings are not measured this way.</li>
  <li>Cyclicals stay, marked: for them the cash is what carries them through the downturn.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro off_radar(r) %}{% call rules("Small, little-followed companies outside the US that score well on both Value and Quality.") %}<ul>
  <li>Outside the US, under ${{ r.radar_max_cap }}B of market value, followed by {{ r.radar_max_analysts }} analyst{{ '' if r.radar_max_analysts == 1 else 's' }} at most.</li>
  <li>Value &ge; {{ r.radar_min_value }}, Quality &ge; {{ r.radar_min_quality }}, return on capital &ge; {{ r.radar_min_roic }}%.</li>
  <li>Revenue per share compounding at {{ r.radar_min_compounding }}%/yr or more, owner earnings not falling, no dilution, recent numbers held.</li>
  <li>No financial services or real estate, nothing cyclical or at peak margins.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro carry(r) %}{% call rules("High, growing dividends with no tax withheld for foreign investors, covered by what the business earns.") %}<ul>
  <li>No dividend withholding tax for a foreign individual (statutory rate): the dividend is kept whole.</li>
  <li>Yield of {{ r.carry_min_yield }}% or more, and above what a euro margin loan costs today, {{ r.carry_borrow_rate }}% (the ECB's euro short-term rate plus 1.5% of broker margin and 0.5% of safety, read at each update), so it could service a loan.</li>
  <li>Covered by the owner yield: what is not paid out stays in the company and keeps growing its value.</li>
  <li>The dividend record, payment by payment: no cut in the last {{ r.carry_unbroken }} complete years nor in the last twelve months, and the regular dividend per share no lower than ten years ago. A company without that record is left out: a dividend bought on a loan needs the evidence.</li>
  <li>Owner earnings per share trended up over at least {{ r.carry_min_years }} annual reports (one dip allowed).</li>
  <li>Little debt inside the company, since a loan to buy it adds leverage on top: net debt at most {{ r.net_debt }}&times; operating profit or interest covered {{ r.cover }}&times;, and at most {{ r.net_debt_equity }}% of equity (banks, insurers and holdings: equity at least {{ r.equity }}% of assets).</li>
  <li>Not cyclical, not at peak margins. Borrowing to buy adds risk: the loan, its interest and the price stay yours.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro dividend_growers(r) %}{% call rules("A dividend held for at least a decade and growing faster than inflation, covered by owner earnings, in a lasting business with little debt.") %}<ul>
  <li>No cut in the regular dividend per share for at least {{ r.grower_years }} complete years, nor in the last twelve months (the twelve months below the twelve before, with as many payments in each).</li>
  <li>Judged payment by payment, in the currency the dividend is declared in. A special dividend (a payment at least {{ r.special_ratio }}&times; its season's usual size, higher than the same payment a year before and a year after) is left out, so the year after it is not a cut. A payment that slipped across New Year, or moved earlier when an interim dividend was added, is no cut either. Any other fall of more than {{ r.dividend_held }}% is.</li>
  <li>Dividend yield of {{ r.grower_yield }}% or more; the regular dividend per share growing {{ r.grower_growth }}% a year or more over 10 years; the dividend at most {{ r.grower_payout }}% of owner earnings.</li>
  <li>Normal return on capital (on equity for banks and insurers) of {{ r.grower_roic }}% or more.</li>
  <li>Lasting: not cyclical, not at peak margins, past the AI screen, the dividend not cut more than {{ r.recession_fall }}% in a past recession ({{ r.recessions }}, and {{ r.downturns }}).</li>
  <li>Little debt: net debt at most {{ r.net_debt }}&times; operating profit or interest covered {{ r.cover }}&times;, and at most {{ r.net_debt_equity }}% of equity (banks and insurers: equity at least {{ r.equity }}% of assets).</li>
  <li>It keeps only the best {{ r.top }} of the combined ranking; a bank or an insurer, ranked apart, the best {{ r.top }} of its own kind.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro shortlist(r) %}{% call rules("Boring companies worth researching in depth: dull, essential businesses in industries that do not attract new capital and that AI cannot easily replace; cheap, good, cleanly reported, and through stress tests for recession and debt.") %}<p>At most {{ r.n }} companies passing every test the figures allow, at once. A test whose figure is missing is passed.</p><ul>
  <li><strong>Growth without dilution</strong>: revenue (book for banks, insurers and holdings) per share growing, shares not issued, owner earnings per share not shrinking, at least {{ r.incremental }}% earned on the capital added.</li>
  <li><strong>Cash</strong>: free cash flow positive in at least {{ r.fcf }}% of the years.</li>
  <li><strong>Clean accounts</strong>: Beneish M-score (a test for manipulated earnings) at most {{ r.beneish }}; accruals (profit not backed by operating cash) at most {{ r.accruals }}% of assets.</li>
  <li><strong>Debt</strong>: net debt at most {{ r.net_debt }}&times; operating profit (EBIT) or interest covered {{ r.cover }}&times; (banks, insurers and holdings: equity at least {{ r.equity }}% of assets); net debt at most {{ r.net_debt_equity }}% of equity, so higher rates and inflation can be carried; the gross margin now no more than {{ r.max_margin_loss }} points below where it was in the year of the highest inflation, so costs can be passed on.</li>
  <li><strong>Holding up</strong>: analysts' estimates not cut by more than {{ r.estimate_cut }}% in 90 days; recent revenue and operating profit not down more than {{ r.revenue_fall }}% and {{ r.ebit_fall }}%; no dividend cut in the last twelve months.</li>
  <li><strong>Stress tests</strong>: no industry naturally fragile or exposed to AI (software, IT services, consulting, staffing, advertising, publishing, education, business services, internet content, gaming, health information, entertainment, broadcasting); where a global recession hits hard, and for any cyclical, only the industry's leaders (top {{ r.leader_share }}% by revenue among the benchmark's companies, the top {{ r.leader_min }} always) holding net cash; among banks only the leaders by assets.</li>
  <li><strong>A boring industry</strong>: one that does not attract new capital, so the incumbents face few new competitors: {{ r.boring_industries }}.</li>
  <li><strong>Price</strong>: an owner yield of at least {{ r.hurdle }}%, the higher of 6% and the 10-year Treasury (a business should pay more than a bond), plus how much more than the US the company's home government pays to borrow dollars (its rating-based default spread, from Damodaran's January table, less the US's, in points: {{ r.spreads }}; the country of its headquarters, not of its sales); Value &ge; {{ r.value }}.</li>
  <li><strong>Quality</strong>: normalised return on capital (on equity) of {{ r.roic }}% or more, Quality &ge; {{ r.quality }}.</li>
  <li><strong>Grade</strong>: a stricter moat and management bar than the other lists': both grades at least {{ r.grade_both }} <em>and</em> one of them {{ r.grade_top }} or more (the other lists: both at least {{ r.grade_both }}, or either at least {{ r.grade_one }}).</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}

{% macro repeaters(r) %}{% call rules("Companies that several screened lists pick at once across distinct styles, and that pass the moat and management grade.") %}<ul>
  <li>On two or more of the screened lists across at least two distinct styles (so the two 20-year compounder lists or the two balance-sheet lists alone do not make a repeater), ordered by how many lists (most first), then by the combined rank. Hover the Lists number to see which.</li>
  <li>Each screened list counts every company that qualifies and meets that list's own moat and management grade bar; the pure score rankings ({{ r.repeater_ranked }}) are left out.</li>
  <li>Only companies whose moat and management grade passes the bar (both at least {{ r.grade_both }}, or either at least {{ r.grade_one }}); ungraded ones are graded before they can enter.</li>
  <li>Many of them are among the Boring companies too: the same answer, reached from several directions. Banks, insurers and REITs are judged apart, on one list of their own each, so a repeater is in practice an operating company.</li>
</ul>{% endcall %}{% endmacro %}
